UnboundLocalError: local variable &lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&apos;matplotlib&apos;&lt;/span&gt; referenced before assignment
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which made me thing that on a new machine, there was no &lt;tt&gt;matplotlib&lt;/tt&gt; installed!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And indeed, after installing it - I&apos;m able to run a paragraphs again.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How do you think, would it make sense to add some explicit dependency checks for matolotlib at the beginning, that can fail in a user-friendly manner like we did in `py4j` - &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/apache/zeppelin/blob/aded8681bb06e2ca897253820640b153afa3c468/python/src/main/resources/bootstrap.py#L116&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://github.com/apache/zeppelin/blob/aded8681bb06e2ca897253820640b153afa3c468/python/src/main/resources/bootstrap.py#L116&lt;/a&gt;  or is there a better way to verify the dependencies ?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15662333" author="agoodman" created="Sun, 13 Nov 2016 23:44:51 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;That&apos;s actually not supposed to happen, the `_setup_matplotib()` method should simply exit when matplotlib cannot be imported. I think the approach of just checking for imports at the python level is fine as the behavior is correct in pyspark but apparently for python I used a &lt;tt&gt;pass&lt;/tt&gt; statement instead of &lt;tt&gt;return&lt;/tt&gt;. A very silly mistake indeed. I&apos;ll fix this right now.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
